You are absorbing a completed role-play scene into a campaign chronicle and evolving its records.
Read the transcript and reply with ONLY a JSON object, no prose around it, with keys:

"one_line" (a one-sentence summary),
"summary" (one self-contained paragraph),
"keywords" (list of significant nouns/concepts, lowercase),
"timeline_events" (list of {"date","text"} for concrete datable HAPPENINGS; [] if none),
"character_state_edits" (list of {"id","current_state","knows","suspects"} — for each present character whose standing snapshot changed, the FULL rewritten snapshot: "current_state" is their standing condition, "knows" is what they now hold as certain, "suspects" is what they believe but have not confirmed; drop what is no longer true, standing facts only (not a running log). Use "" for a field that does not apply),
"group_state_edits" (list of {"id","goals","resources","focus","public_perception","secrets"} — for each group from the "Groups:" context line whose standing changed this scene, the FULL rewritten sections; include only the fields you are rewriting: an omitted field keeps its stored value, an explicit "" clears it; standing facts only, not a running log),
"lore_edits" (list of {"id","append"} — a paragraph to add to a lore/location entry),
"authored_edits" (list of {"id","field","text"} — ONLY when a character's core card field (description/personality/scenario) fundamentally and durably changed; rare),
"relationship_deltas" (list of {"from","to","trust","affection","tension","note"} — for each directed pair whose feelings changed, the FULL updated values; use the "<kind>:<id>" tokens from the context block; trust/affection/tension are 0-5),
"bond_changes" (list of {"a","b","type"} — a shared relationship type for a pair),
"plot_movements" (list of {"id","title","status","beat"} — for each plot thread this scene moved: the thread id from the context block to advance or close it, or a NEW thread (omit "id", give a "title"); "status" is one of open/advanced/closed; "beat" is one sentence on how this scene moved it; only threads that actually moved),
"commitment_movements" (list of {"id","title","kind","status","due","beat"} — for each COMMITMENT the scene opened or moved: something the story now owes its reader. "kind" is one of promise/threat/foreshadowing — a promise is what a character undertook to do, a threat is harm someone has been told is coming, foreshadowing is an image or detail planted to pay off later. Give the id from the context block to move an existing one, or omit "id" and give a "title" to open a new one. "status" is one of open/fulfilled/broken/expired — a commitment RESOLVES, so use "fulfilled" when it was kept or paid off, "broken" when it was reneged on or failed, "expired" when the story has moved past it unpaid, and "open" while it still stands. "due" is the deadline the fiction itself states, in the story's own words ("before the thaw", "by the third night") — omit it when nothing was stated, and never invent one; give it as "" when this scene lifted a deadline the commitment already had without resolving the commitment itself. "beat" is one sentence on what this scene did to it; only commitments this scene actually touched. This is not the same list as plot_movements: a plot thread advances, a commitment is kept or broken),
"new_characters" (list of {"name","description","history","personality","mes_example","evidence","confidence","open_questions","sd_prompt"} — ONLY for a person who is named and speaks or acts in the scene but has NO existing character record, i.e. is not already one of the ids in the "Present:" context line; "evidence" is one sentence naming what the transcript actually showed about them; "confidence" is exactly one of thin/sketched/established, where thin means the scene showed only a small functional role, sketched means the scene showed some motive/voice/relationship, and established means the scene showed enough recurring-person depth to treat them as solid campaign cast; "open_questions" is a short list or sentence of unknowns future play should resolve; "description" must be a complete, richly detailed W++ block, e.g. [character("Name") { Species("...") Age("...") Occupation("...") Personality("trait", "trait", ...) Appearance("...", "...") Likes("...", ...) Dislikes("...", ...) Speech("...") Goals("...") }], covering everything the scene reveals and filling the remaining slots with specific inventions consistent with it — never leave a slot generic or empty, but keep inventions compatible with the confidence level; "history" is the character's backstory, 1-3 paragraphs of third-person prose — expand what the scene establishes into a concrete past (origins, formative events, how they came to be where the scene finds them), inventing plausible specifics where the scene is silent; "personality" is a prose paragraph on temperament, motivations, fears, mannerisms and how they treat others, consistent with the W++ block but not a copy of it; "mes_example" is example dialogue — 2 or 3 short exchanges, each opening with a line reading <START>, with lines prefixed "{% raw %}{{user}}{% endraw %}:" and "{% raw %}{{char}}{% endraw %}:" (this character is {% raw %}{{char}}{% endraw %}), capturing their distinctive voice, diction and verbal tics as shown in the scene; "sd_prompt" is a detailed comma-separated Stable Diffusion prompt of PHYSICAL APPEARANCE ONLY — sex and apparent age, build, skin tone, hair color/length/style, eye color, facial features, and any distinguishing marks (scars, tattoos, freckles), at least 8 specific tags — it must NEVER mention clothing, outfits, armor, jewelry or other accessories, since outfits are generated separately and composed on top; propose one only when the scene gives enough material for a real entry, not for every passing name),
"new_locations" (list of {"name","body","keys","sd_prompt","current_setting"} — for a named place mentioned that has no existing location record; "body" is a short descriptive paragraph; "keys" is a comma-separated list of words that should trigger this entry in future scenes; "sd_prompt" is a suggested Stable Diffusion prompt for the location; "current_setting" is true for at most the ONE new location that is where this scene's action actually took place, false otherwise),
"new_lore" (list of {"name","body","keys"} — for a new group, item, piece of history, or concept mentioned that isn't a person or a place; same "keys" convention as above),
and "weather_edits" (list of {"location","condition","temperature","wind","duration_blocks","note"} — ONLY when the narration actually states the weather, and only for the axes it states: "it was raining" gives a condition and says nothing about wind, so leave the others out. Omit "location" for the scene's current place, or give a location id for somewhere else the scene describes. Weather is otherwise generated, so do not report a sky the transcript did not mention. "duration_blocks" is how long the narration says it lasts, counted in five-per-day time blocks — omit it for weather that is simply happening now, and give it only when the text states an extent ("the rain set in for three days" is 15). "note" is the phrase from the transcript that established it).

Write in third person, past tense. Use the ids given in the context block.
